WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.520
Most of Ras sits on the interface of the plasma membrane.

2
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.640
Yet Ras has no stretch of amino acids that crosses a membrane.

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:12.360
So what holds it there?

4
00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:17.820
Ras relays a growth signal inward from a receptor, so where it sits matters.

5
00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.140
The answer is a fatty chain.

6
00:00:21.917 --> 00:00:26.497
Cells attach fatty chains to proteins, using dedicated enzymes.

7
00:00:27.217 --> 00:00:29.417
On Ras, that chain is called Pharnasale.

8
00:00:29.917 --> 00:00:33.797
A long chain is comfortable buried in a membrane, and uncomfortable in water.

9
00:00:34.417 --> 00:00:37.717
But one short chain is a weak grip, and chains pop out again.

10
00:00:38.297 --> 00:00:42.757
A protein held this way visits a membrane and leaves it, still tied to its chain.

11
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:47.030
So cells add a second signal, often one of two kinds.

12
00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:52.910
One kind is a second fatty chain, as in H -RAS, which carries farnesyl plus

13
00:00:52.910 --> 00:00:56.850
palmitate. The other kind is a patch of positive charge.

14
00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:01.150
K -RAS 4B carries farnesyl plus a run of lysines.

15
00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:05.150
Those lysines are attracted to the negative lipids of the interface.

16
00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:11.193
Binding free energies add, which means affinities multiply.

17
00:01:11.813 --> 00:01:17.713
Each extra 1 .4 kilocalories per mole at body temperature, makes binding about 10

18
00:01:17.713 --> 00:01:21.593
times tighter. So two weak grips make one strong grip.

19
00:01:22.173 --> 00:01:27.133
Think of hooks. One hook holds a coat until someone bumps it, and two hooks hold it.

20
00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:32.587
The second signal is also a handle for control: remove it and the protein loosens

21
00:01:32.587 --> 00:01:38.587
its hold. In K-Ras4B, protein kinase C puts a phosphate next to the lysines.

22
00:01:39.187 --> 00:01:42.227
The phosphate adds negative charge beside a positive patch.

23
00:01:42.727 --> 00:01:48.027
The attraction to the membrane weakens, and K-Ras4B moves to internal membranes.

24
00:01:48.487 --> 00:01:50.827
This is called a pharnosol electrostatic switch.

25
00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:57.510
H-Ras carries a second fatty chain, palmitate, joined by a bond that is easily cut.

26
00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:01.050
Enzymes called thioesterases cut that bond.

27
00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:05.110
So palmitate is the one chain cells can put on and take off.

28
00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:09.670
Once it is gone, the grip weakens, and H-Ras drifts away.

29
00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:15.950
So keep this rule: one chain visits, a second signal holds, and removing that signal

30
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:16.950
loosens the grip.
